meta {
  name: Create order
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/orders
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{partnerKey}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "external_order_id": "PO-{{$timestamp}}",
    "external_customer_id": "C-77",
    "amount": "500.00",
    "currency": "USD",
    "customer": {
      "email": "pat@example.com",
      "phone": "+6580000000",
      "full_name": "Pat Lim"
    }
  }
}

docs {
  You send an **amount**; the system composes a real basket of active SKUs totalling it exactly.

  `external_order_id` is the idempotency key. Send the same one twice with the same body and you
  get the original order back with `Idempotent-Replay: true` and a 200 instead of a 201 — which
  is how you tell a retry from a new order. Send it with a *different* body and you get
  `ORDER_IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`, because answering with either order would be wrong.

  `amount` is a string with exactly two decimals. A JSON number would lose a cent somewhere
  between your client and ours.

  Refusals worth handling: `AMOUNT_OUT_OF_RANGE` (422), `EXACT_COMPOSITION_UNAVAILABLE` (422 —
  no basket totals that amount, so try a different one), and `COMPOSITION_TIMEOUT` (503, and the
  only one marked retryable — a second attempt gets a different seed and may well work).
}
